+
+### SC02-R2 — foreground registration outlives reusable process identity
+
+The frozen first candidate 152467880098cd87581ca279be4d2bc872a7620b retains a
+Capture after observing its direct child's reap, until its readers are joined.
+A numeric PID can be reused during that interval. Its unconditional map insert
+can therefore replace an older incomplete invocation, hiding owned workers from
+stop. Record this as FIX before changing the existing registry.
+
+Retain one existing foreground registry, keyed by an internal non-reusable
+invocation identity or an equivalent representation retaining every generation.
+PID is process data, not the lifetime identity. Every ordinary and streamed
+foreground registration/removal/handoff must carry its own identity; older
+completion cannot replace or erase a newer invocation. Stop must enumerate all
+retained captures and refuse clean completion while any required capture worker
+has not been observed. Keep signaling under the owned Child's unreaped-state
+check. If allocating identity can fail, refuse before spawn; never wrap or reuse
+a live or retired identity. Add no public protocol field, dependency or registry.
+
+A controlled registration seam may present a reused numeric PID while real
+owned children and capture state establish older reaped-but-incomplete custody.
+Require both invocations retained, generation-specific completion/removal,
+truthful bounded stop and later successful cleanup. This proves the internal
+identity invariant, not that OS PID wraparound occurred in an executed run.
+Retain all existing producer/consumer, stop/race/disconnect and wire assertions.
+
+The existing pre-aborted control proves bounded observed abort and its result
+shape; it does not establish that the child installed its TERM-ignore trap before
+the abort. Keep that narrower claim and the separate timeout/escalation control.
+Active nonblocking-reader wakeups remain an unmeasured performance/soak question.
+Neither nonblocking observation independently starts a fix round.
+
+SC02-R2 stream-stop integration: stop must not signal a cached stream PID after
+the request may have reaped its Child. The actual stream request may consume
+the existing runtime stopping flag and signal only while it still owns an
+unreaped Child. Keep its invocation registration through observed completion
+or an admitted background handoff, serialize affected spawn/handoff against
+stop admission, and refuse bounded clean stop while a required foreground
+registration is unresolved. This is the same unique registry and existing
+request owner; no extra process table, public cancellation field or retry.
+
+SC02-R2 exceptional stream ownership: once stream spawn succeeds, every early
+return or unwind must retain the actual Child and all started reader handles.
+A setup/wait failure cannot leave only an inert marker after dropping those
+resources. The request must perform bounded cleanup or transfer real cleanup
+custody into its exact invocation in the same registry, where stop can observe
+later completion. Keep unobserved worker handles owned, and remove registration
+only after actual reap plus required worker observation. Normal completion or
+background handoff relinquishes custody only after its admitted transition.
+Use real setup-error fixtures and actual entered worker/child controls; an
+injected wait/unwind boundary is labeled controlled, not an OS failure replay.
+No second registry, runtime, protocol field or generic background controller is
+introduced. The separate existing background raw-PID stop window remains open
+for a following existing-owner batch and blocks full Box/shipping acceptance.
+
+SC02-R2 completed-recovery observation: exceptional cleanup must retain unresolved
+resources, not indefinitely retain already reaped, worker-free registrations or
+parent pipe handles. After bounded exceptional cleanup, nonblockingly observe
+finished readers and remove only the exact completed invocation. Reuse existing
+foreground request boundaries to observe retained stream recoveries whose readers
+finish just after the exceptional return; add no sweeper thread, scheduler or
+second registry. An observation error or still-live child/reader remains pending.
+The request outcome stays unknown even after resource cleanup becomes complete.
+
+Require real setup failure and repeated-error controls proving actual child
+termination/reap without accumulating completed registrations until stop. Keep a
+late-reader recovery retained through real EOF, then prove an existing normal
+request boundary observes it without using shutdown as a garbage collector.
+Fixture-only observation may expose the actual owned state without substituting
+execution results. Keep inherited pipes alive by an owned explicit release until
+the unresolved assertion; an independent countdown cannot supply that oracle.
+Retain failure cleanup and all prior identity, unknown outcome and no-resignal
+assertions. Normal stream drain/termination and full background custody remain
+separate open behavior; this correction does not accept them.
